  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some showers around into tonight before a tranquil \nstretch takes over.\n\nA closed low will continue to gradually shift eastward and exit off \nthe New England coast this evening. The cyclonic flow remaining over \nour area along with lingering cool air aloft is resulting in quite a \nbit of cloud cover this afternoon. This looks to continue for a \nwhile tonight before some thinning starts to occur. More clearing \nmay occur sooner across parts of Delmarva however. A shortwave will \nrotate across our area into this evening and this will support some \nshowers. While a few lightning strikes cannot be ruled out into this \nevening, there is currently meager MLCAPE and the visible satellite \ntrends so far are not all that supportive for thunderstorms. As a \nresult, opted to remove thunder from the forecast especially given \nthe even lower probability. The visible satellite imagery this \nafternoon also shows a weak low spinning southward off the New \nJersey coast. This feature may enhance the shower development some \nalong portions of the New Jersey coast. A northwesterly breeze will \nease into this evening.\n\nSurface high pressure will then begin to settle in during tonight \nwith some drier air. Some guidance hinting at a few rather isolated \nshowers Friday afternoon, however given the closed low/trough \ncontinuing to move away, opted to continue with a dry forecast. Some \nwarming aloft along with some increase in sunshine Friday will \nresult in warmer afternoon temperatures. Lighter flow however will \nresult in a sea/bay breeze developing by later in the afternoon. \nDepending on how far inland this gets by early Friday evening, some \nincrease in the dew points should occur in its wake. Temperatures \nmoderate some more to start the weekend with high temperatures on \nSaturday right near average along with dry conditions. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A frontal system on Sunday and Monday will bring the \nnext opportunity for widespread showers and thunderstorms.\n\nHigh pressure weakens late Saturday and is replaced by advancing low \npressure from the Ohio Valley area. This low strengthens and moves \nwest of our area later Sunday and into Monday. This system will \nbring increasing chances for showers and a few tstms Sunday \nafternoon for the western counties and all areas Sunday night and \nMonday. The PWAT values off the forecast soundings during these \ntimes show more 2+ inch PWAT values for our region, so it's possible \nthat excessive rainfall may develop for parts of the region. Latest \noutlook from WPC shows a marginal risk of excessive rainfall. \nMoisture and instability will also increase ahead of the front, so \nwe likely will have to monitor the severe weather threat for the \nSunday and Monday afternoon periods as well. However, our area is \nnot in any long term severe outlooks, so there is a chance that \nthere are more hydro concerns with this system.",
